WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.850
Good morning, Hank, it's Wednesday, and my day went like this. I got up at 5:30 a.m.

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:06.600
because apparently babies don't know about time zones and Birmingham is one hour behind Indianapolis,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.300
even though longitudinally, they are basically indistinguishable from one another,

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.220
which is the kind of thing that really annoys me at 5:30 in the morning. Stupid time zones.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.520
Then I made some coffee and felt better.

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:19.640
And then since I was up I figured I might as well drive down to my high school, Alma Mater,

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.519
Indian Springs School.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.240
So my first novel, Looking For Alaska,

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.600
is about a guy from Florida who is obsessed with the dying words of famous people

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.840
and then leaves home to go to a boarding school in Alabama.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.060
And I, myself was once a guy from Florida who was obsessed with the dying words of famous people

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.200
and then left home to go to a boarding school in Alabama.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.180
Arrrrgh

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.040
traffic jam.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.400
it took me like half an hour to get through this.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.380
But you're gonna get through it instantaneously.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.120
Anyway, Looking For Alaska is fictional,

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:42.620
but the setting really isn't.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:44.699
The school in the book is called Culver Creek

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.720
but it is almost inch for inch the same place that Indian Spring School was in 1995.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.020
So, it's always a little weird to go back because there's the interstate exit,

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:54.420
and there's Sunny Convenience Kiosk

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.320
and there's Kusa Liquors where they cater to your spiritual needs

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:58.640
and here is the swan

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.080
and the octagonical cafeteria

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.100
and the beautiful view from the classroom windows

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.900
and here is the swing where Alaska and Miles have their first conversation,

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.620
where Alaska tells Miles the last words of Simón Bolivar:

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:10.260
"Dammit!

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.140
How will I ever get out of this labyrinth?"

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.940
So it's still summer, which means no students

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.960
and also that it is hot, like 103 degrees.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.480
But just as they say: it isn't the heat so much as it's the humidity.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.440
Actually it's also the heat.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.049
So the school was quiet in a way that it never got during the school year and for a minute,

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:26.970
I felt like I was walking through a ghost town

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.380
but then I realized that I was the ghost.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.140
There were only two places I really needed to see,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.880
so I walked past the fancy new dorms

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.139
and past the student-run vegetable garden

00:01:35.139 --> 00:01:37.140
and then down to the first place, this bridge over Bishop's Creek.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.140
In both Looking For Alaska and real life,

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.600
the concrete outcropping beneath this bridge was known as the

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.919
"Smoking Hole."

00:01:42.919 --> 00:01:44.540
I could tell that it had been a while since anyone had been here

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.960
because the briars had grown into a tangle over the path.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.480
I hadn't been down here in 15 years,

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.299
but it looked the exact same as my memory, except with no cigarette butts.

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:53.800
"Maybe," I thought at first, "people just don't smoke anymore."

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:54.479
Which would be awesome.

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:57.440
But then I started to worry that this slab of rock had simply been forgotten.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.600
That there'd been a break in the secret line of communication

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.080
that handed down the smoking hole from one generation to the next.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.280
And that made me feel old,

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.200
like I was in love with a place that had been forgotten.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.100
And I was feeling pretty sorry for my old-ass self

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:07.949
when I happened to look up and see

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.140
an Emily Dickinson quote graffiti'd on the wall,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.760
apparently in 2008.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.060
Well you can imagine the nostalgia,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.820
Hank, down there where I have loved and lost,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.340
staring down at this creek that ran through my adolescence,

00:02:17.349 --> 00:02:20.420
feeling comforted by the knowledge that our legacy was well maintained

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.140
by the students of today with their Dickinsonian graffiti.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:26.780
And I'm walking back up at the very pinnacle of this fit of sentimentality

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.980
and then bam!

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.360
Stung by a bee.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:30.820
Ahhhhhh

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:31.460
Bee sting.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:32.120
Bee sting.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:32.780
Bee sting.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.020
Owwwww.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:36.880
Ahhhhhh.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.040
Aw, that hurts.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.800
Awwwww daddy.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:42.970
That's always how it goes, Hank.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.260
You're just trying to romanticize your frickin' past for one second

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.919
and then a frickin' bee comes along and stings you on the very top

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.069
of your left butt cheek,

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:50.509
ruining everything.

00:02:50.509 --> 00:02:52.660
Of course, then I had to call Sarah and tell her I'd been stung by a bee

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.519
because it is a medically proven fact

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.520
that the only thing that lessens the pain of a bee sting

00:02:57.620 --> 00:02:58.820
is whining to your wife.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.340
These trampolines are just close enough to each other to invite poor decisions

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.319
and just far enough apart to punish those poor decisions.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:07.200
Eventually I found myself in the second place,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.200
there used to be a barn in this field,

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.400
a barn where I first told a girl I loved her

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.880
and where I spent my first all-nighter studying world history by flashlight,

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.160
while drinking astonishingly bad wine.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.820
Emily Dickinson wrote that: "Success is counted sweetest by those who ne'er succeed

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:25.120
and so too youth is counted sweetest by those who are no longer young."

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.540
Nostalgia is inevitably a yearning for a past that never existed

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.500
and when I'm writing there are no bees to sting me out of my sentimentality.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.899
For me at least, fiction is the only way I can even begin to twist my lying memories into something true.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.780
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

